WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.506
EF Core raw SQL features are useful in some cases, but they are higher-review areas.

2
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:14.355
Teams may use raw SQL for performance, complex queries, provider-specific capabilities, existing stored procedures, or reporting needs.

3
00:00:14.355 --> 00:00:24.319
Those reasons can be valid, but raw SQL should be limited, documented, and reviewed because it moves developers closer to the database command boundary.

4
00:00:24.319 --> 00:00:30.415
Values supplied to raw SQL APIs should be handled through safe provider-supported mechanisms.

5
00:00:30.415 --> 00:00:37.953
The central rule remains the same: application-defined command structure should stay separate from variable values.

6
00:00:37.953 --> 00:00:46.212
Reviewers should be able to see where values are bound, where structure is chosen, and why raw SQL is necessary for that path.

7
00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:51.456
Dynamic filtering, sorting, reporting, and export features deserve special care.

8
00:00:51.456 --> 00:00:56.175
They often accept flexible caller options and may touch broad data sets.

9
00:00:56.175 --> 00:01:08.564
A safe design maps caller choices to approved internal query shapes instead of allowing arbitrary field names, expressions, fragments, or table-like choices to flow into database structure.

10
00:01:08.564 --> 00:01:15.447
Stored procedures can provide a useful database boundary, but they are not automatic security guarantees.

11
00:01:15.447 --> 00:01:23.771
A procedure can still contain unsafe dynamic construction, broad permissions, sensitive logging, or weak ownership assumptions.

12
00:01:23.771 --> 00:01:31.834
The application and database teams should review procedure behavior, parameters, permissions, and operational use together.

13
00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:36.226
Dynamic SQL inside stored procedures still requires careful design.

14
00:01:36.226 --> 00:01:49.139
The same defensive questions apply inside the database: which parts are approved structure, which parts are values, how are values bound, who can call the procedure, and what objects can it affect.

15
00:01:49.139 --> 00:01:54.973
The fact that logic is inside a procedure does not remove the need for safe construction.

16
00:01:54.973 --> 00:01:57.660
Clients should not control SQL structure.

17
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:07.165
They may choose supported filters, statuses, ranges, or report options, but the application should translate those choices into approved queries.

18
00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:15.489
Building query features around approved shapes gives product teams flexibility without making data access impossible to review.
